# Avoid If-Chains in Services & Actions

The codebase deliberately eliminates branching in services via design patterns (see `Documentation/ARCHITECTURE.md` → "Service Refactoring Patterns"). New code must follow the same rules.

## Signals you're drifting

- More than ~2 `if` branches shaping the return value of a service method.
- Repeated `if (user.Mfa)` / `if (channel == "sms")` / `if (lang == "ar")` style switches.
- Guard clauses mixed with business branches in the same method.

## Preferred alternatives

| Pattern              | Use for                                                 | Project example                              |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|
| Strategy + Factory   | Many implementations keyed by a value (channel, method) | `IMfaMethodStrategy` + strategy factory      |
| Builder              | Composing an output step-by-step                        | `LoginResponseBuilder`, `ClaimsBuilder`      |
| Resolver             | Chain of fallbacks (language → fallback language)       | `TranslationResolver`                        |
| Validator helpers    | Multiple guard-style precondition checks                | `UserValidator.ValidateCredentials(...)`     |
| Pattern matching     | Type-based or shape-based dispatch                      | `return entity is IEntity e ? e.Id : null;`  |

## Example — before / after

```csharp
// ❌ Before
if (admin == null) throw new UnauthorizedException("Invalid credentials");
if (!_hash.Verify(password, admin.Password)) throw new UnauthorizedException("Invalid credentials");
if (admin.Status != UserStatus.Active) throw new UnauthorizedException("Account not active");
if (admin.Mfa) return BuildMfaResponse(admin);
return BuildStandardResponse(admin);

// ✅ After
_authService.ValidateCredentials(admin, password);           // guards via validator
return _loginResponseBuilder.ForUser(admin!, "admin").Build(); // branch via strategy
```

## Allowed exceptions

- A single early-return guard clause is fine (`if (dto.RoleIds is null) return;`).
- Pattern matching with 2–3 arms is preferred over new types; promote to Strategy once there are 3+ real branches with behavior.
